1. OOP Fundamentals

Think of OOP as modeling a system using objects that contain state + behavior.

Concept	Simple meaning	Java example	Interview point
Abstraction	Expose what something does, hide how	PaymentProcessor interface	Interfaces/abstract classes
Encapsulation	Keep data + behavior together and control access	private balance + deposit()	Protect invariants
Inheritance	Child gets behavior/state from parent	Dog extends Animal	is-a relationship
Polymorphism	Same interface, different implementations	payment.pay() → UPI/Card	Runtime polymorphism
Association	Two objects interact/know each other	Teacher ↔ Student	Generic relationship
Aggregation	Weak ownership	Team has Players	Child can survive parent
Composition	Strong ownership	House has Rooms	Child lifecycle tied to parent
Abstraction

Hide implementation details and expose only necessary behavior.

public interface PaymentProcessor {
    void pay(double amount);
}

public class UpiPaymentProcessor implements PaymentProcessor {
    public void pay(double amount) {
        // UPI-specific implementation
    }
}

Caller only knows:

processor.pay(1000);

It doesn't care how UPI payment happens.

Interview: Abstraction reduces complexity and allows implementations to change independently.

Encapsulation

Restrict direct access to internal state.

class BankAccount {

    private double balance;

    public void deposit(double amount) {
        if (amount <= 0)
            throw new IllegalArgumentException();

        balance += amount;
    }

    public double getBalance() {
        return balance;
    }
}

Bad design:

public double balance;

Now anybody can do:

account.balance = -10000;

Encapsulation protects business invariants.

Inheritance

Represents an IS-A relationship.

class Vehicle {
    void start() {}
}

class Car extends Vehicle {
}

Car IS-A Vehicle.

Inheritance gives code reuse, but creates tight coupling between parent and child.

Polymorphism

One abstraction → multiple implementations.

interface NotificationSender {
    void send(String message);
}

class EmailSender implements NotificationSender {
    public void send(String message) {}
}

class SmsSender implements NotificationSender {
    public void send(String message) {}
}

Usage:

NotificationSender sender = new EmailSender();
sender.send("Order placed");

Later:

sender = new SmsSender();

Caller doesn't change.

This is heavily used in LLD through Strategy, Factory, State, Command etc.

Composition vs Inheritance

One of the most common LLD interview discussions.

Inheritance:

class ElectricCar extends Car {
}

Composition:

class Car {

    private Engine engine;

    Car(Engine engine) {
        this.engine = engine;
    }
}

Now:

new Car(new PetrolEngine());
new Car(new ElectricEngine());

The idea behind:

Favor composition over inheritance.

Composition is usually preferable because behavior can be changed, tested and extended independently.

Inheritance → is-a

Composition → has-a

For example, Spring Boot itself heavily encourages composition:

@Service
class OrderService {

    private final PaymentProcessor paymentProcessor;

    OrderService(PaymentProcessor paymentProcessor) {
        this.paymentProcessor = paymentProcessor;
    }
}

Instead of:

class OrderService extends PaymentProcessor

which makes no conceptual sense.

Association vs Aggregation vs Composition

Think in terms of ownership.

Association
Teacher -------- Student

Aggregation
Team ◇---------- Player

Composition
House ◆---------- Room

Association: objects simply interact.

Doctor ↔ Patient

Aggregation: parent contains child, but child can exist independently.

Team → Players

Delete Team → Players still exist.

Composition: strong ownership.

Order → OrderItems

Delete Order → normally its OrderItems should also disappear.

A simple interview rule:

Association   → uses/knows
Aggregation   → has-a, weak ownership
Composition   → has-a, strong ownership
Inheritance   → is-a

+    public
-    private
#    protected

────────>   Association
◇───────    Aggregation
◆───────    Composition
△───────    Inheritance